<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oun Kham</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1811">1811</a>–<a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a> was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a> in the late 19th century, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Siam. In 1887 Chinese Haw bandits and their Tai allies sacked his capital, and the French vice-consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auguste-pavie">Auguste Pavie</a> helped the elderly king escape down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>. The episode strengthened French claims, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-siamese-war">Franco-Siamese crisis of 1893</a> the kingdom came under the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oun-kham.jpg" alt="Oun Kham" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Auguste Pavie (1847-1925) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ounkham_(cropped_1).gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
